.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "September 2026" "conn 0.7" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- every project and its processes, in one window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn restart
.br
.B conn \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A terminal UI. It lists each open project's shells, dev servers and agents, and
marks the ones waiting for input. macOS and Linux. Processes keep running when
the window is closed.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBconn\fR is a terminal UI for working on many projects at once. It knows every
git repository under your project roots and what is running in each, and it
treats a process the way an editor treats a file: every shell, agent, task and
container log it holds is a buffer, the buffers open are the tabs across the top
of the window, and the ones that need you are marked.
.TP
1-2.
conn is one window. conn's own pane runs across the top, two rows: the tabline,
and the heading of the buffer shown. The buffer with focus fills the window
under it; the tmux status line sits at the foot. The other buffers keep running
out of view until you step back to them. Close the terminal and everything keeps
running; run \fBconn\fR again and it shows what it showed.
.TP
1-3.
Install the release built for this machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ curl -fsSL https://conn.w0zro.com/install.sh | sh
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-4.
The script checks the download against the release's checksums, puts the binary
in \fB~/.local/bin\fR and the manpage under \fB~/.local/share/man\fR. \fBman
conn\fR is this manual.
.PP
.I "Table 1-1. Install script overrides"
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_INSTALL_DIR\fR
where the binary goes
.TP
\fBCONN_MAN_DIR\fR
where the manpage goes
.TP
\fBCONN_VERSION\fR
a release other than the latest
.RE
.TP
1-5.
From source, with Go 1.25 or later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ go install github.com/w0zro/conn@latest
.fi
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
conn needs \fBtmux\fR installed: \fBbrew install tmux\fR, or your package
manager's. On macOS it also needs \fBlsof\fR, which macOS ships. \fBdocker\fR is
optional: with it, a project's containers are listed too (Section 6).
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ conn
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-6.
There is nothing to set up. With no buffer open, the window is the everything
view (2-3), which shows what is running, so on a quiet machine it says
\fBnothing running\fR and names the doors: \fB⌃p\fR find a project · open a
buffer, \fB.\fR show all, \fB?\fR the keys. \fBctrl\-p\fR is the finder (3-2):
type a project's name, and \fBenter\fR opens a shell there. conn looks under
\fB~/projects\fR; Section 9 points it elsewhere.
.SH THE WINDOW
.TP
2-1.
The tabline is the working set: one tab for every buffer conn holds — the
shells it opened, the agents, the tasks, a plan's entries, a container's logs
— named place/name: \fBconn/claude\fR, \fBdatum/tests\fR, \fBpg\fR. The tab
with focus is bold ink on the ground; the others are gray. Each tab carries its
buffer's mark (Table 2-1), and the mark stays while the state does. The right
end holds one hint for the moment: \fB⌃p opens\fR, \fBr reruns\fR, \fBesc
keeps it\fR, \fB. toggles running · all\fR.
.PP
.I "Table 2-1. Marks, on a tab and on a row"
.RS
.TP
\fB⠇\fR
in amber: working — an agent mid-turn
.TP
\fB◆\fR
in owed orange: an ask owed — a permission prompt, a question
.TP
\fB●\fR
in green: done, and waiting on you
.TP
\fB✓\fR
in green: the command ended well. Beside the name, what the run said of itself
and how long ago: \fBtest · 12 passed · 2h\fR
.TP
\fB✗\fR
in owed orange: the command ended badly, or the process is stopped or hung:
\fBtest · 3 failed · 3m\fR
.TP
\fB⬢\fR
in teal: a container
.RE
.TP
2-2.
Under the tabline is the heading of the buffer shown: its name, bold in
parchment, then its facts in gray, joined by middots. \fBin <place>\fR; the
command a task ran; its pid; the ports it listens on; an agent's branch and the
size of its context, \fB48.2k tokens\fR, read from its transcript; how long a
waiting agent has waited, \fBwaiting 3m\fR. For a run that ended, how, in the
mark's color: \fB✗ exit 1 · 3 failed · 2m ago\fR, or the same after ✓;
then its past runs from the record (Appendix B), newest first: \fBpast runs ✗
2m ✓ 12s ✓ 14s\fR. What a buffer is doing is read off its heading.
.TP
2-3.
\fB.\fR at conn, or \fBctrl\-space \-\fR from any buffer, is the everything
view: every process, grouped by place, drawn by conn in the whole window under
the tabline, where an \fBeverything\fR tab stands first. It is a tree, at most
four levels deep: \fBgroup\fR → \fBrepository\fR → \fBsub\-project\fR →
\fBprocess\fR. A group, a repository and a sub-project are each a place: a row
you can open a shell at. A repository is any git repository under a root. conn
skips \fBnode_modules\fR, \fBvendor\fR, \fBtarget\fR, \fB.build\fR, \fBPods\fR,
\fB.venv\fR, \fBvenv\fR, every directory whose name begins with a dot, and
whatever \fBskipDirs\fR names.
.TP
2-4.
A folder inside a root that holds two or more repositories is a group. It is a
place of its own: \fBs\fR opens a shell there, \fBr\fR runs its plan, \fBx\fR
covers everything beneath it.
.TP
2-5.
In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest is a sub-project and gets a row under
its repository. A manifest is any of \fB.conn\fR, \fBProcfile\fR,
\fBpackage.json\fR, \fBdeno.json\fR, \fBcomposer.json\fR, \fBgo.mod\fR,
\fBCargo.toml\fR, \fBGemfile\fR, \fBmix.exs\fR, \fBpyproject.toml\fR,
\fBpom.xml\fR, \fBbuild.gradle\fR or \fBbuild.gradle.kts\fR. Directories git
ignores are skipped; an untracked \fB.conn\fR still counts. A process working in
a directory with a manifest is listed under it either way: the process makes the
sub-project, and the manifest names it.
.TP
2-6.
Each place lists what is running in it, named by what you would call it: \fBnpm
run dev\fR, not \fBnode\fR. A row that listens carries its port, \fBnpm run dev
· :5173\fR; an agent's row carries its model; a container's row its mark and
its age, \fB⬢ pg · :5432 · up 2d\fR; a shell a plan or a task started is
named for its entry; a row that ended or is stuck carries the mark of Table 2-1.
Place headings are bold in parchment, with the branch; the cursor is an orange
▸; a folded place says what it hides, \fB+3 folded\fR. Faint rows have no
buffer to step into: processes started outside conn, and processes a shell's
command started in turn, a claude's tools, say; the view says so of them, \fBdim
means look, don't step\fR. \fBenter\fR reaches as far as the shell above them.
All are listed and killable. The row that is conn itself says \fB(me)\fR. The
newest conversation at rest under a place with work is a row of its own,
\fBclaude · suspended · 6h\fR (5-3); what runs outside every project is under
\fBglobal\fR (6-5). The foot of the view teaches two lines of keys; with nothing
running it says \fBnothing running\fR and names the doors (1-6).
.TP
2-7.
The status line at the foot begins with the \fBCONN\fR chip — bold, the
ground's color on the orange, the one inverted ground on the screen — then one
mode chip, bold in its color, while one holds (Table 2-2). Then, in gray,
anything conn has to say until the next key: a report, an error, or that a newer
release is out, \fBconn 0.6.0 is out · U installs it\fR; else the session's
facts, \fBdatum/tests · 3 buffers · 12 more processes live behind them\fR, or
in the everything view \fB17 processes · 6 places\fR. What a chord says from a
buffer shows there too, for a few seconds. The right end is \fB? keys\fR.
Nothing else lives there.
.PP
.I "Table 2-2. Modes on the status line"
.RS
.TP
\fBPREFIX\fR
in amber: ctrl-space pressed, waiting for the next key
.TP
\fBCOPY\fR
in teal: scrolling back through a pane
.TP
\fB/query\fR
a query being typed, or the filter standing
.TP
\fBALL\fR
in teal: the everything view is up
.TP
\fBCONFIRM\fR
a kill, or \fBR\fR, waits on its second key
.TP
\fBFAILED\fR
the shown buffer's run ended badly
.TP
\fBN OWED\fR
answers owed: agents waiting, runs failed, processes stuck
.TP
\fBWORKING\fR
the shown buffer's agent is mid-turn
.RE
.TP
2-8.
The mouse works throughout: the wheel scrolls back through a pane, a drag
selects and copies on release, a click focuses the pane under it, and a program
that uses the mouse gets its own clicks and wheel. Hold shift to select with the
terminal's own selection. A program that copies for itself, vim's clipboard yank
for one, reaches the system clipboard. A click into conn's pane at the top
focuses it, and the keys of Appendix A work there; \fBctrl\-space \-\fR brings
the everything view up from any pane.
.TP
2-9.
conn draws in truecolor with a palette of its own, the hangar (Table 2-3). Hue
is spent on state and focus alone, one accent to a phrase. Every pane sits on
the hangar's ground and ink unless the configuration's \fBtheme\fR says
\fBterminal\fR, which leaves the shells the terminal's own colors; conn's pane,
the status line and the popups are the hangar either way.
.PP
.I "Table 2-3. The hangar"
.RS
.TP
\fBground · #15130F\fR
behind every pane; wash #1D1A15 behind the status line and the popups; chip
#2A2620 for chips, borders and the cursor's bar
.TP
\fBink · #E6DFD0\fR
text; gray #8B8272 for facts and tabs without focus; faint #5C564A for rows conn
cannot step into
.TP
\fBparchment · #BFB39A\fR
headings and place names
.TP
\fBorange · #E85D2F\fR
the brand, the cursor and focus
.TP
\fBowed · #FF7847\fR
asks, failures and dying
.TP
\fBamber · #E3A94F\fR
working
.TP
\fBgreen · #93C98B\fR
done and passed
.TP
\fBteal · #7FC7BD\fR
identity: ports, branches, containers, the names of variables
.RE
.SH MOVING
.TP
3-1.
Between buffers, at conn — in its pane, or in the everything view; from a
buffer, the same keys after \fBctrl\-space\fR (3-7):
.PP
.I "Table 3-1. Moving between buffers"
.RS
.TP
\fBJ K\fR
the next and previous buffer, in the tabline's order
.TP
\fBtab\fR
the next thing owed (3-8)
.TP
\fBshift+tab\fR
back: the previous buffer, or everything (3-9)
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open the buffer under the cursor
.TP
\fBp ctrl\-p\fR
the finder (3-2)
.TP
\fB.\fR
the everything view; there, running or all
.TP
\fBq\fR
leave; everything keeps running (10-1)
.RE
.TP
3-2.
\fBctrl\-p\fR, from any buffer, opens the finder: the front door. \fBp\fR or
\fBctrl\-p\fR at conn, and \fBctrl\-space p\fR, \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-p\fR and
\fBctrl\-space /\fR from anywhere, open it too. It is a popup over the window: a
query line with an orange › prompt, and under it one list of everything
openable (Table 3-2). Each word of the query must appear in order inside what a
row says, not contiguously: \fBtsl\fR finds tressle-api, \fBmono api\fR finds
the api inside mono, and the letters that matched are lit in orange. The
cursor's row is a bar with an orange ▸; \fB↑↓\fR move it.
.PP
.I "Table 3-2. What the finder lists"
.RS
.TP
\fBa buffer held\fR
with its state and last word: \fB● running · done, review when ready\fR,
\fB✗ 1 failed · 2m\fR
.TP
\fBa task not running\fR
every place's test, build and lint, with the last runs: \fBtask · last runs ✓
12s ✗ 2m ✓ 14s\fR
.TP
\fBa plan's entry not running\fR
\fBplan · npm run dev\fR
.TP
\fBa container\fR
\fB⬢ container · :5432 · up 2d\fR
.TP
\fBa conversation at rest\fR
the newest under each place: \fBdatum · resume\fR, the last prompt, its age,
its branch (5-3)
.TP
\fBa place\fR
\fBdemo/terminal — shell · opens at the root\fR
.TP
\fBa process conn cannot step into\fR
dim: look, don't step
.RE
.TP
3-3.
\fBenter\fR attaches to a buffer that is running; starts what is defined — a
task, a plan's entry — and a task whose last run lies dead in its buffer is
run again in the same pane; opens a container's logs as a buffer, \fBdocker
compose logs \-f\fR; continues a conversation at rest where it was had; opens a
shell at a place. A query that matches nothing offers to make the project:
\fBenter\fR on \fBw0zro/parser\fR makes the repository under the first projects
root and opens its shell (4-2). \fBctrl\-a\fR starts an agent where the cursor's
row is; \fB,\fR turns the kind of agent (5-4); \fBesc\fR closes. The foot of the
popup says as much: \fBno match? enter on \(dq<query>\(dq makes the repo and
opens its shell · agents: ^a a claude here · , the next kind\fR. conn writes
the listing beside the socket whenever it changes (Appendix B), so the finder
opens at once; with no conn running it reads the machine itself.
.TP
3-4.
In the everything view:
.PP
.I "Table 3-3. Keys in the everything view"
.RS
.TP
\fBj k\fR
move; the arrows move too
.TP
\fBgg G\fR
top and bottom
.TP
\fBspace\fR
fold or unfold the place under the cursor; a folded place says \fB+n folded\fR
.TP
\fB\-\fR
unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids, and a container's id
.TP
\fB.\fR
everything, or only what is running
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open the row's buffer
.TP
\fBs a r\fR
a shell, an agent, the plan, at the place
.TP
\fBt b l\fR
its tests, build, lint (Section 7)
.TP
\fBx X\fR
preview a kill of the row, or of its tree (Section 8)
.TP
\fBe\fR
the environment (4-3)
.TP
\fBU R\fR
update conn; end the server (Section 10)
.TP
\fBesc\fR
clear the filter, else close the view
.RE
.TP
3-5.
\fB/\fR narrows the view by anything a row says. Each word of the query must
appear in order inside it, not contiguously, and the letters that matched are
lit. A place answers by its name; a process by anything its row says: the
command, its arguments, the plan's name for it, an agent's name and model, a
port. Processes that answer are listed under their places, pruned to the
branches that answer. An empty query lists places alone.
.TP
3-6.
A search is a way of getting somewhere, so the keys that go somewhere work while
the query is still being typed (Table 3-4). Acting ends the search; the filter
holds, so the subject stays listed. \fBesc\fR cancels the search and puts the
cursor back where it was. The query takes readline's editing keys:
\fBctrl\-w\fR, \fBctrl\-u\fR, \fBctrl\-h\fR, \fBctrl\-e\fR and the rest.
.PP
.I "Table 3-4. Keys while a query is typed"
.RS
.TP
\fB↑↓\fR
move through what is left
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open a shell on a place; open a found process's buffer
.TP
\fBctrl\-r\fR
run the plan there
.TP
\fBctrl\-a\fR
start an agent there
.TP
\fBctrl\-x\fR
preview a kill of what the cursor is on
.TP
\fBesc\fR
cancel the search
.RE
.TP
3-7.
\fBenter\fR on a buffer's tab or row, or \fBs\fR on a place, focuses the buffer:
it fills the window under the tabline. From then on the buffer receives every
keystroke but two: \fBctrl\-p\fR, the finder, and \fBctrl\-space\fR, the prefix
that reaches conn from any buffer (Table A-2). \fBctrl\-space \-\fR brings the
everything view up. \fB?\fR, at conn or after ctrl-space, lists every key in a
popup over the window; Appendix A lists them here.
.TP
3-8.
\fBtab\fR at conn, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR anywhere, goes to the next thing
owed: an agent waiting, a run that ended badly, a process stopped or hung. Again
continues around them. A held buffer is shown, at its prompt or transcript; a
process conn only watches gets the cursor in the everything view. The view is in
the same order: places by name and each place's rows by name, except that a row
that is owed something stands first among its siblings and a place with one
stands above the places without, so what is owed is at the top of the view as
well as under \fBtab\fR.
.TP
3-9.
\fBshift+tab\fR at conn, or \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR anywhere, returns
focus to where it was: the buffer before this one, or the everything view when
focus came from it. Again returns. Before focus has moved at all, it is the
everything view from a buffer and the cursor's buffer from the view.
.SH SHELLS
.TP
4-1.
\fBs\fR on a place in the everything view, \fBenter\fR on a place in the finder,
or \fBctrl\-space s\fR from a buffer, opens your shell at the place: a buffer,
with a tab in the tabline, focused, filling the window. When another buffer
takes focus it moves out of view, where it keeps running, and its tab stays.
Each shell keeps 10,000 lines of scrollback; \fBscrollback\fR in the
configuration raises that.
.TP
4-2.
The finder makes projects. A query that matches nothing is offered as a name:
\fBenter\fR on \fBw0zro/parser\fR makes \fBw0zro/parser\fR under the first
projects root, folder and all, runs \fBgit init\fR in it and opens a shell
there, and the project is listed from then on. A directory already there is left
alone. \fBesc\fR closes the finder instead.
.TP
4-3.
\fBe\fR on a buffer, or \fBctrl\-space e\fR from any pane, is the environment,
annotated: a popup over the window, headed \fB<name>'s environment · pid 4402
· 61 variables, 6 telling\fR. The process is the one the pane is running —
the dev server, not the shell that started it — and the variables are the ones
it was started with; a shell at its prompt shows what it started with, and an
export typed since is not there. The telling variables come first: the
\fB*_ENV\fR family, \fBPORT\fR and \fBHOST\fR, \fBLOG_LEVEL\fR, URLs and DSNs,
\fBVIRTUAL_ENV\fR; each is \fBNAME=value\fR, the name in teal, and after a
\fB←\fR in gray what conn read from it, \fB← where it listens\fR: in owed
orange when the value looks wrong, \fB← nothing is listening\fR, in amber when
it looks suspicious, a venv from another project. Each is read for what it is. A
path says whether it is there; PATH is counted, its missing and repeated entries
marked, and the place's tools found along it. A URL or a port that points at
this machine says who is listening there, or that nothing is. Secrets are never
listed; a password in a URL is masked \fB•••\fR. \fBspace\fR unfolds all N
variables, grouped by who set them: the project's — the ones its \fB.env\fR
sets, a variable the file names that the process lacks being a line of its own
— then the runtime's, npm's, conn's, the shell's rc files, the terminal conn
was attached from. For a variable a startup file set, the page says which file
and line: it starts the shell once more, traced, after its first paint, and
reads the assignment off the trace; a variable the trace did not see set is
looked for in the startup files by name, and said to be named there. An entry of
PATH that \fB/etc/paths\fR put there says so. \fB/\fR finds, \fBesc\fR closes.
On a place the page is the server's environment, which every shell starts from:
the terminal's, brought over each time one attaches, so a variable its rc files
stopped exporting is gone from the next shell opened. \fBconn env\fR typed at a
prompt shows that shell's environment as it is now, exports and all, since the
command inherited it. On macOS a system shell at its prompt keeps its
environment to itself; the page says so and shows what the shell started from,
and \fBconn env\fR typed there shows its own.
.SH AGENTS
.TP
5-1.
\fBa\fR on a place in the everything view, \fBctrl\-a\fR in the finder, or
\fBctrl\-space a\fR from a buffer, starts an agent at the place, in a buffer
like any other: Claude Code, unless the configuration's \fBagent\fR says
otherwise. Claude Code reports its state, and its transcript says whether it has
answered since it started, so a finished turn is read from the instance rather
than remembered by the window, and stands across a restart of conn; conn marks
each instance, on its tab and on its row:
.PP
.I "Table 5-1. Agent marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB⠇\fR
working
.TP
\fB◆\fR
blocked mid-turn: a permission prompt, a question
.TP
\fB●\fR
finished its turn; waiting for the next ask
.TP
\fB○\fR
idle since it started
.RE
.TP
5-2.
The ◆ is the one to answer first: answering it resumes work already underway.
The answer is typed in the buffer itself, at the agent's own prompt, and the
mark clears when the instance says the ask is answered. A waiting instance's
heading says how long it has waited, \fBwaiting 3m\fR, beside its branch and the
size of its context; the finder's row for it says the last word. The status line
counts the asks owed while any is. \fBtab\fR, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR from
any buffer, goes to it (3-8).
.TP
5-3.
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended, and can be
picked back up. The newest conversation at rest under each place is a row of the
finder, \fBdatum · resume\fR, with the last thing asked of it, how long it has
rested and its branch; \fBenter\fR continues it in a buffer opened where it was
had. In the everything view the same conversation is a row of its own, dimmed,
at the end of the place's family, \fBclaude · suspended · 6h\fR, and
\fBenter\fR on it continues it. A conversation an instance still carries is not
offered. A place with no work shows none there: the row is kept the way an
exited container is kept beside its running siblings.
.TP
5-4.
conn knows two kinds of agent: \fBclaude\fR and \fBollama\fR. \fB,\fR at conn or
in the finder, or \fBctrl\-space ,\fR from any buffer, moves \fBa\fR on to the
next kind, around the end, for every window until conn is restarted; the
finder's foot names it, \fB^a a claude here\fR. Ollama's rows are plain
processes, unmarked. It runs the model most recently pulled or run; with nothing
pulled, \fBa\fR runs \fBollama list\fR instead, and with the ollama server down
the REPL says so. \fBagentRuns\fR in the configuration names what a kind runs
when its own choice is not the wanted one.
.SH PLANS
.TP
6-1.
Working on a project usually means the same processes every time. A project
lists them in \fB.conn\fR, Procfile format, one name and command per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-1. A plan"
.TP
6-2.
Without one, conn reads the project's own files, in this order:
.PP
.I "Table 6-1. Where a plan comes from"
.RS
.TP
\fB.conn\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBProcfile\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
one entry: its \fBdev\fR script, else its \fBstart\fR script
.TP
\fBcompose.yaml\fR
one entry, \fBcompose: docker compose up\fR, since compose runs every service
and attaches to each; \fBcompose.yml\fR, \fBdocker\-compose.yml\fR and
\fBdocker\-compose.yaml\fR the same
.RE
.TP
6-3.
\fBr\fR on a place, \fBctrl\-space r\fR from a buffer, or \fBenter\fR on an
entry in the finder, starts the entries that are not running, each in a buffer
named for it, with a tab in the tabline, and shows the first. An entry is
running when its command is running in the place, whoever started it: the shell
\fBr\fR opened for it, a shell opened by hand, or a terminal outside conn. The
command a shell was started with is kept on it, so what a shell ran is what it
ran whatever the plan says since. It is a list to run, not a supervisor: nothing
restarts on its own. When an entry's command ends, it says so itself as it goes,
and conn hears it rather than finding the shell at its prompt on a later look;
the shell stays at the prompt with the transcript, the tab and the row show ✗
or ✓, the heading says how and when it ended and the runs before it, and
\fBr\fR starts it again beside the old shell. What the run said of itself, and
that its ending is on the record, are kept on the shell, so a conn started later
says the same and records nothing twice. Running anything by hand in that shell
clears the mark.
.TP
6-4.
A project that runs its services with docker compose shows each service as a
row: named for the service, with the ports it publishes, \fBweb · :8438\fR,
under the \fBdocker compose up\fR that runs it when one is running in a shell,
and under the place itself when compose runs detached. Its row carries the
container's mark, ⬢ in teal, and in the finder its port and its age, \fB⬢
container · :5432 · up 2d\fR. \fBenter\fR on a service, in the view or the
finder, opens its logs as a buffer, \fBdocker compose logs \-f\fR, with a tab
marked ⬢; the heading names the container, its image and its status. A service
that died stays listed while the project runs, with the cross and how long ago,
\fBworker · 3m ✗\fR, and \fBtab\fR goes to it; one that exited well shows the
check. A service whose health check is not passing shows that in place of its
ports: \fBapi · unhealthy ✗\fR, or starting.
.PP
.RS
.nf
compose-demo
   app
     ⬢ cache · :6390
     ⬢ web · :8438
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-2. A compose project's services, under the compose that runs them"
.TP
6-5.
What runs outside every project is listed under \fBglobal\fR, at the foot of the
everything view, while there is anything to list: containers started by hand or
from elsewhere, processes listening on a port from outside your projects, an
\fBollama serve\fR or a \fBbrew\fR postgres, and agents started outside your
projects, with what they run: an agent is yours wherever it runs, and the place
is a label on it, not a gate. The system's own listeners are left out. \fBx\fR,
\fBenter\fR and \fBe\fR work there as under a project; there is no directory, so
no shell opens there and no project is made.
.TP
6-6.
What \fBx\fR stopped, \fBr\fR brings back where it was: the services the compose
file declares that are down are started with \fBdocker compose up \-d\fR, and a
compose up running in a shell picks up their logs again. An entry \fBr\fR is
starting that runs compose itself brings everything up instead. A service that a
plan entry is named for is left to the entry.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A plan is not a supervisor. An ended entry's buffer stays at its prompt, with
its transcript, until \fBx\fR closes it or \fBr\fR starts the entry again beside
it.
.SH TASKS
.TP
7-1.
\fBt\fR, \fBb\fR and \fBl\fR run the place's tests, build and lint as the
project itself defines them. What the project wrote down comes first: a Makefile
target, a justfile recipe, a Taskfile task or a package.json script named
\fBtest\fR, \fBbuild\fR or \fBlint\fR. Otherwise the ecosystem's own command.
Only a linter the ecosystem ships with is guessed at; a project on ruff or
eslint names it in a script. A project the guess is wrong for says so in its
Makefile, which conn checks first.
.PP
.I "Table 7-1. How a task's command is found"
.RS
.TP
\fBMakefile, justfile, Taskfile\fR
the target, recipe or task named for the task
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
the script named for the task, run with npm
.TP
\fBgo.mod\fR
\fBgo test ./...\fR, \fBgo build ./...\fR, \fBgo vet ./...\fR
.TP
\fBCargo.toml\fR
\fBcargo test\fR, \fBcargo build\fR, \fBcargo clippy\fR
.TP
\fBpyproject.toml\fR
\fBpytest\fR; \fBpython3 manage.py test\fR for a Django project
.TP
\fBGemfile\fR
\fBrspec\fR with a spec directory, else \fBrake test\fR
.TP
\fBmix.exs and the rest\fR
the ecosystem's own: mix, gradle, mvn, deno, swift, dune
.RE
.TP
7-2.
Each run is a buffer named for its task, \fBdatum/tests\fR, in a shell of its
own, so its tab and its row show ✓ or ✗, \fBtab\fR goes to a failure, and
the status line's chip says \fBFAILED\fR while a failed one is shown. The buffer
outlives its run — dead, but readable: the transcript stays, and the heading
says how the run ended and the runs before it, \fB✗ exit 1 · 3 failed · 2m
ago · past runs ✓ 12s ✓ 14s\fR. Stepping onto a dead buffer, by \fBtab\fR,
\fBJ K\fR or the finder, shows it with the keys kept at conn: \fBr\fR runs it
again in place — the same pane, cleared, the same name; \fBgf\fR opens the
last file:line the transcript names in \fB$EDITOR\fR, vi without one, in the
buffer's own shell; \fBq\fR closes the buffer, its runs staying on the record;
\fBenter\fR steps into the shell beneath. Running anything by hand in that shell
makes it a live buffer again, and the ending clears. The key again replaces a
finished run rather than stacking another beside it; a run still going is left
to finish. \fBconn test\fR, \fBconn build\fR, \fBconn lint\fR and \fBctrl\-space
t\fR, \fBb\fR, \fBl\fR do the same from any buffer, and the finder lists every
place's tasks not running, with their last runs.
.TP
7-3.
conn reads the run's summary line as the tool prints it: go test, pytest, jest,
vitest, mocha, cargo, rspec, ExUnit, golangci-lint, tsc, ruff, eslint. A failed
run's heading says \fB✗ exit 1 · 3 failed\fR, a passed one \fB✓ 12
passed\fR, and its row says the same. Output conn does not recognize leaves them
with pass or fail alone.
.SH STOPPING
.I "Table 8-1. Kill keys"
.RS
.TP
\fBx\fR
preview a kill of the shown buffer's process, or of the row under the cursor
.TP
\fBX\fR
preview a kill of it, everything under it, and what it started that has since
detached
.TP
\fBx on an entry\fR
end the entry's command; the buffer stays, and \fBr\fR starts the entry again
.TP
\fBx on a container\fR
stop the container
.TP
\fBx on a place\fR
preview a kill of everything running in it
.TP
\fBx on a signalled row\fR
kill outright: SIGKILL to what has not gone
.TP
\fBR\fR
end conn and every buffer it holds
.RE
.TP
8-1.
Nothing dies unasked. \fBx\fR shows, in the whole window, what dying means
before anything dies: the heading, \fB<name> in <place> · about to die\fR; the
process tree with ✗ before each name, and after it the pid and the ports it
holds, \fBpid 4431 · holds :3000\fR; the consequences in prose, \fBthe tree
dies together — an orphaned esbuild is a zombie you would meet again as ✗
stopped · :3000 frees · nothing else touches it\fR; and the confirm line, \fBX
kills all 3 · x the head alone · esc changes your mind\fR. The status line's
chip says \fBCONFIRM\fR, and beside it the facts, \fBconn/web · 3 processes ·
:3000\fR. \fBX\fR takes the tree; \fBx\fR the head alone, the row's process and
the shell around it; \fBy\fR or \fBenter\fR whichever was asked. Processes get
SIGTERM; \fB9\fR confirms with SIGKILL, \fBi\fR with SIGINT, for a server that
only tears down on ctrl-c, and \fBh\fR with SIGHUP, for a daemon that reloads on
it. Any other key keeps everything alive and gives the buffer back. A signalled
row keeps its mark until the process is seen to have gone; conn never claims an
exit it has not seen. A process that has not gone in five seconds is given up
on, and the status line says so; \fBx\fR on it then, or on a row still on its
way out, offers SIGKILL: outright. Kills work on any listed process, including
ones conn did not start.
.TP
8-2.
After death the buffer stays as the record. A held shell running a command is
not hung up: the command is signalled, and the shell sits at its prompt beneath
the transcript with the ending recorded, how and when, so a \fBdocker compose
up\fR stops its containers on the way out and its buffer remains to read;
\fBq\fR closes it (7-2). A held shell at its prompt with nothing running is hung
up: a buffer of nothing but a prompt. \fBx\fR on an entry's row previews the
entry, named, and once it is dead \fBr\fR starts it again. \fBX\fR, and \fBx\fR
on an entry, also kill processes the command started that have since detached
from it; such a process is listed under the command while it runs, and the
preview says \fBorphaned\fR of it.
.TP
8-3.
A container is stopped by docker, which signals the process inside it and, after
a grace period, kills it. \fBr\fR brings the service back (6-6).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
9-1.
One JSON file, entirely optional, at \fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR;
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is honored.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  \(dqprojectsDirs\(dq: [\(dq~/projects\(dq, \(dq~/work/mono\(dq],
  \(dqscrollback\(dq: 50000,
  \(dqtheme\(dq: \(dqterminal\(dq,
  \(dqskipDirs\(dq: [\(dqbazel-out\(dq, \(dqdist\(dq]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 9-1. A configuration"
.TP
9-2.
Every field has a default; a file that names none is the same as no file.
.PP
.I "Table 9-1. Configuration fields"
.RS
.TP
\fBprojectsDir\fR
the directory searched for repositories; ~ and $VARS expand (default
$HOME/projects)
.TP
\fBprojectsDirs\fR
several roots; when set, projectsDir is ignored. A root that only exists on
another machine is fine
.TP
\fBscrollback\fR
lines of scrollback each shell keeps. Raising it takes a restart: \fBR\fR, then
\fBconn\fR again, or \fBconn restart\fR (10-2) (default 10000)
.TP
\fBnavWidth\fR
read and ignored: there is no column now
.TP
\fBtheme\fR
hangar, the palette of 2-9 on every pane, or terminal, which leaves the shells
the terminal's own colors (default hangar)
.TP
\fBagent\fR
the kind of agent \fBa\fR starts, until \fB,\fR says otherwise: claude or ollama
(default claude)
.TP
\fBagentRuns\fR
what starting a kind runs, by name: {\(dqollama\(dq: \(dqollama run mistral\(dq}
.TP
\fBskipDirs\fR
directory names never searched, on top of the built-in list
.RE
.SH LEAVING, RESTARTING, UPDATING
.TP
10-1.
\fBq\fR at conn, or \fBctrl\-space q\fR anywhere, leaves conn and returns you to
the terminal; on a dead buffer \fBq\fR closes the buffer first (7-2), and the
next \fBq\fR leaves. Everything keeps running: the shells, the plans, the
agents. Run \fBconn\fR again, from any terminal, and it shows what it showed. If
conn's pane was closed, \fBctrl\-space \-\fR brings the everything view, and the
pane with it.
.TP
10-2.
\fBR\fR, or \fBctrl\-space R\fR from any buffer, ends conn and every buffer it
holds, after asking. \fBconn restart\fR, typed in a terminal outside conn, does
the same and then starts conn again; inside conn it stops with a message, since
the shell running it would go too. A restart is the way to a fresh start, and
what a raised \fBscrollback\fR needs to take effect.
.TP
10-3.
conn checks for a newer release once a day. When one is out, the status line
says so whenever nothing else is being said: \fBconn 0.6.0 is out · U installs
it\fR. \fBU\fR installs it and restarts conn as the new build; the buffers stay.
With nothing newer known, \fBU\fR checks now and says what it finds. Installing
a new binary by hand works the same: the next \fBconn\fR picks it up. A build
from source is never told it is behind.
.TP
10-4.
conn also answers on the command line. From a shell inside conn, these act on
the directory the shell is in:
.PP
.I "Table 10-1. Commands"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn nav\fR
conn itself, the program in the home window's top pane
.TP
\fBconn home [key]\fR
the everything view; handed a key, that key pressed at conn — \fBx\fR for the
kill preview of the focused buffer
.TP
\fBconn shell [dir]\fR
a shell in dir, as a buffer
.TP
\fBconn agent [dir]\fR
an agent in dir, as a buffer
.TP
\fBconn kind\fR
the next kind of agent, for \fBa\fR and the agent chord
.TP
\fBconn run [dir]\fR
the plan of the place holding dir
.TP
\fBconn test [dir]\fR
its tests; \fBbuild\fR and \fBlint\fR the same way
.TP
\fBconn jump\fR
the next thing owed
.TP
\fBconn back\fR
back: the previous buffer, or everything
.TP
\fBconn next, prev\fR
the next and previous buffer
.TP
\fBconn finder [client]\fR
the finder, in a popup over the client; \fBconn page finder\fR is the page
inside it
.TP
\fBconn keys [client]\fR
the key list, in a popup; \fBconn page\fR is the page inside it
.TP
\fBconn env [pid client]\fR
the environment of the pane whose shell is pid, annotated, in a popup; \fBconn
page env pid\fR is the page inside it
.TP
\fBconn page [env pid | finder]\fR
the page a popup shows: the keys, an environment, the finder
.TP
\fBconn ls\fR
the held shells: pid, directory, name, exit; one per line
.TP
\fBconn restart\fR
end conn, after asking, and start it again
.RE
.TP
10-5.
\fBconn ls\fR is for scripts: what the everything view lists, in its order, one
run per line, tab-separated: the pid of the run's head, the place, the name the
row shows, its state (\fBworking\fR, \fBblocked\fR or \fBwaiting\fR for an
agent; \fBfailed\fR, \fBdone\fR, or \fBrunning\fR), the exit of a held shell's
command once it has ended, and the ports the run listens on. It is built from
the same places, processes and held shells as the window, so the two never
disagree. With conn not running no shells are held, and the list is the
processes of the places, which is not an error.
